  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 257 AM EDT Mon Sep 14 2026\n\nToday-Tonight... Surface high pressure slides eastward over the \nGreat Lakes region, pushing a cold front offshore the northeast and \nmid Atlantic coast. Light offshore winds early this morning begin to \nveer after sunrise, turning east-northeast as the east coast sea \nbreeze develops midday. High-res guidance indicates an active \neast coast sea breeze, initiating scattered showers and storms \nprimarily west of I-95 as it pushes inland and towards the western\nhalf of the peninsula through early evening. Primary storm \nhazards remain locally heavy downpours, frequent lightning \nstrikes, and localized wind gusts of 40-50 mph. \n\nAnother hot and humid day is expected. Most locations start the day \nwith temperatures in the low to mid 70s. By the afternoon, \ntemperatures peak just a few degrees above seasonal climatology with \nhighs in the low 90s. Deep moisture persists, and peak heat index \nvalues will spread 102-107 degrees. \n\nTuesday-Thursday... High pressure expands along the eastern seaboard \nas a strong ridge builds aloft and centers over the southeast U.S. \nEast-northeast winds become breezy and gusty Tuesday afternoon, \nfurther increasing to 15-20 mph Wednesday (locally higher along the \ncoast). Breezy conditions continue through Thursday as winds \ngradually subside. At area beaches, building rough surf and a \nhigh risk of rip currents are expected by Wednesday. Heed the \nadvice of lifeguards, beach patrol flags, and signs. Entering the\nsurf is strongly discouraged!\n\nThe increased flow into mid week should generally break down the \npersistent sea breeze pattern that has been driving the local \nweather recently. Deep moisture holds in place Tuesday, favoring \nmoderate to high coverage of showers and storms. A much drier \nairmass becomes advected over portions of north-central Florida \nWednesday, and the greatest rain chances (60-80%) become trimmed \nsouthward towards southern Brevard, the Treasure Coast, and across \ninland areas in vicinity of Lake Okeechobee. Despite the drier \nairmass, moistening of the low levels in onshore flow should at \nleast favor scattered rain chances further north, particularly along \nthe coast. Moisture begins to recover Thursday, returning \nscattered to numerous rain chances more areawide (still highest \nalong the coast). Continuing to watch for a localized heavy \nrainfall threat that may develop along portions of the coast \nThursday as a mid to upper level trough passes westward over the \nstate. \n\nHigh temperatures in the low 90s on Tuesday fall only a few degrees \nWednesday and Thursday, remaining seasonally warm. Low temperatures \nmostly in the low to mid 70s inland will remain warm along the \ncoast, mostly in the upper 70s and near 80 degrees.\n\nFriday-Sunday... The mid to upper level ridge over the southeast \nU.S. gradually breaks down late week as troughing aloft continues to \npush west of Florida and into the Gulf. Southwest to south flow \nbehind the trough aloft will advect a column of deeper moisture over \ncentral Florida. This looks to support continued rain chances \nareawide into the weekend. High temperatures are forecast near \nseasonal values mostly in the upper 80s and near 90 degrees.",
